  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Periodicity of $e^{j\\omega t}$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine whether and with what fundamental period the pure complex exponential $e^{j\\omega t}$ repeats by enforcing $e^{j\\omega(t+T)}=e^{j\\omega t}$ and deriving $T_0=2\\pi/|\\omega|$ for $\\omega\\neq0$ within the generalized exponential signal family.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Periodicity of $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Periodicity of $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first question is simple: when does the pure complex exponential repeat, and what does it mean for a shift to leave the signal unchanged? Start with the simplest nontrivial case, $x(t)=e^{jt}$, and treat periodicity as the existence of a nonzero shift $T$ that leaves the signal exactly the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+T)=x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $e^{j\\omega t}$, that definition becomes the test $e^{j\\omega(t+T)}=e^{j\\omega t}$. The goal is to see that this equality will collapse to a condition on the product $\\omega T$, so the next steps can turn repetition into a period formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega(t+T)}=e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the $\\omega=0$ case in mind as the constant-signal exception; here, the main task is to recognize the repetition test and set up the algebra that will determine the period when $\\omega\\neq0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Periodicity of $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Up the Repetition Condition for $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Show that shifting $e^{j\\omega t}$ by $T$ leads to the condition $e^{j\\omega T}=1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the repetition test from the previous step: a periodic signal must satisfy $x(t+T)=x(t)$ for some nonzero $T$. For $x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}$, write the shifted signal and set it equal to the original signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+T)=e^{j\\omega(t+T)}=x(t)=e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now cancel the common factor $e^{j\\omega t}$ on both sides. What remains is the phase-only condition that must hold for repetition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega T}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $e^{j\\theta}=1$ exactly when $\\theta$ is an integer multiple of $2\\pi$, this is equivalent to $\\omega T=2\\pi k$ for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$. The next step will use the smallest positive choice of $k$ to extract the fundamental period when $\\omega>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Periodicity of $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Fundamental Period for Positive $\\omega$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the fundamental period of $e^{j5t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phase condition from the previous step: a repeated complex exponential must satisfy $\\omega T=2\\pi k$. Here $\\omega=5$, so the smallest positive period comes from the smallest positive integer choice of $k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5T=2\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Choose $k=1$ to get the first positive repetition, then solve for $T$. This gives the fundamental period of the signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $e^{j5t}$ repeats every $\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$ units of time. The period is positive, and this is the smallest shift that leaves the signal unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Periodicity of $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Using $|\\omega|$ for Negative Angular Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the fundamental period of $e^{-j5t}$ and express it using $|\\omega|$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same repetition test as before: a periodic complex exponential must satisfy $\\omega T=2\\pi k$. Here $\\omega=-5$, so the sign changes the phase direction but the period still has to come out positive.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega T=2\\pi k \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -5T=-2\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This gives the smallest positive shift directly. The negative sign in $\\omega$ only changes which phase multiple you land on; it does not change the magnitude of the period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}=\\frac{2\\pi}{|-5|}=\\frac{2\\pi}{|\\omega|}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $e^{-j5t}$ repeats every $\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$ units of time. The minus sign does not change the fundamental period; writing the answer with $|\\omega|$ keeps it explicitly positive.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Periodicity of $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing the Constant Case $\\omega=0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Decide what happens when $\\omega=0$ in $e^{j\\omega t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the endpoint of the same family. Set $\\omega=0$ in $e^{j\\omega t}$ and simplify; the signal becomes the constant $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j0t}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A constant signal does not change under any time shift, so the repetition test holds for every $T>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j0(t+T)}=1=e^{j0t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the nonzero-period formula $T_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{|\\omega|}$ does not apply at $\\omega=0$. The signal is periodic, but it has no unique fundamental period because every positive shift works.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
